<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hangzhou</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lin'an</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hangzhou, known in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> period as Lin'an, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> at the southern end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-canal">Grand Canal</a>. It became the capital of the Southern Song after the loss of northern China to the Jin. It grew into one of the largest and wealthiest cities of its time and fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> during the end of the Song Dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hangzhou.jpg" alt="Hangzhou" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: EditQ · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E6%9D%AD%E5%B7%9E%E9%92%B1%E6%B1%9F%E6%96%B0%E5%9F%8E_4_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Song court re-formed in the south as the Southern Song (1127–1279), with its capital eventually fixed at <b>Lin'an</b> (modern Hangzhou).</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The canal linked Luoyang with Chang'an, with the agricultural centres towards Jiangdu (now Yangzhou) and Yuhang (now <b>Hangzhou</b>), and with the northern frontiers near modern Beijing.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sui-dynasty">Sui Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…fragments; their printers were the first after Bi Sheng to use movable type, in clay and in wood, and their Buddhist canon, mostly completed under Renzong, was printed in more than 3,620 juan at <b>Hangzhou</b> in 1302 under the Mongols for the monasteries of the old Tangut lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-xia">Western Xia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rice farmers built a walled city near present-day <b>Hangzhou</b> with a palace complex and flood defences, and its sharply ranked burials mark it as an early state.</blockquote><a href="/culture/liangzhu-culture">Liangzhu culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most southerners were farmers, many of them tenants of great landlords, and townspeople of cities such as <b>Hangzhou</b>, the former Song capital and the largest city of the Yuan realm.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Mo Li Hua&quot;, &quot;Jasmine Flower&quot;, is a Chinese folk song of the xiaodiao or &quot;short tune&quot; genre, popular in the towns of the Jiangnan region south of the lower Yangtze, around Suzhou, Shanghai and <b>Hangzhou</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/mo-li-hua">Mo Li Hua</a></li></ul></section>
